    "summary": "Scorching Cryptical>Other One, rare Big Boy Pete, tight jams",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a fiery Cryptical Envelopment → The Other One suite and features the seldom‑heard Big Boy Pete early in the set. Strong rhythm work shines on Good Lovin', Cumberland Blues and I'm A King Bee, making it a solid 1970 performance.",
    "review": "On March 1, 1970 at the Family Dog on the Great Highway, the Dead turned in a fun, high‑energy set that still feels fresh for listeners. The highlight is undoubtedly the scorching Cryptical Envelopment → The Other One → Cryptical sandwich, where Jerry’s intense, near‑perfect tone drives an explosive jam that segues seamlessly into Black Peter. Early in the set the rare “Big Boy Pete” pops up, giving fans a tasty rarity sandwiched between strong mid‑set numbers like Morning Dew and Hard To Handle. The rhythm section stays solid throughout – Good Lovin' gets a drum‑focused lift, while Cumberland Blues and I’m A King Bee showcase tight, blues‑y interplay. Though the first two songs suffer from audience‑mic bleed and vocals sit a touch high in the mix, an EQ pass brings the soundboard’s clarity back into focus, making this recording the most complete snapshot of the night.",
